Q: Getting error with using Backslash in Python RE A: <p>Your regular expression is correct. What you are missing is the escape character (one more backslash) in your test_string.</p>
<p>Following code gives you the answer &quot;Yes&quot; for your if condition:</p>
<pre><code>import re
test_string = &quot;334-33\\4455&quot; # Have added one more backslash
match_string = r&quot;^[0-9]{3}-{1}[0-9]{2}[\\]{1}[0-9]{4}&quot;
if re.match(match_string, test_string):
  print(&quot;Yes&quot;)
else:
  print(&quot;No&quot;)
</code></pre>

Q: How does xcb&#39;s screen_of_display function work? A: <p>For starters let's determine what is the purpose of the function.</p>
<p>The function iterates over all the screens and once it gets the one you want it returns it.</p>
<p>Also relative to the function implementation it will be important to point to the meaning of the data member <strong>rem</strong> used in the expression <strong>iter.rem</strong>:</p>
<p><code>int rem</code>: The number of remaining screens left to iterate over. When <code>rem == 0</code>, the iteration is complete.</p>
<p>Now consider the for loop used within the function.</p>
<pre><code>    xcb_screen_iterator_t iter;

    iter = xcb_setup_roots_iterator (xcb_get_setup (c));
    for (; iter.rem; --screen, xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter))
    //... 
</code></pre>
<p>The variable <strong>iter</strong> used within the loop is declared before the loop. You may rewrite the loop the following ways.</p>
<pre><code>    xcb_screen_iterator_t iter;

    for (iter = xcb_setup_roots_iterator (xcb_get_setup (c)); 
         iter.rem; 
         --screen, xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter))
    //... 
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>    for (xcb_screen_iterator_t iter = xcb_setup_roots_iterator (xcb_get_setup (c)); 
         iter.rem; 
         --screen, xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter))
    //... 
</code></pre>
<p>The last record of the for loop differs from the preceding records of the for loop by the scope of the declaration of the variable <strong>iter</strong>. In the last declaration of the for loop the scope of the variable <strong>iter</strong> is the scope of the for loop. Outside the for loop the variable is invisible. In other records of the for loop the scope of the variable <strong>iter</strong> is the inner scope of the function.</p>
<p>That is according to the C Standard the for loop is defined the following way</p>
<p>for ( expression<sub>opt</sub> ; expression<sub>opt</sub> ; expression<sub>opt</sub> ) statement</p>
<p>for ( declaration expression<sub>opt</sub> ; expression<sub>opt</sub> ) statement</p>
<p>To make it clear pay attention to that in the second definition of the for loop the declaration part  implicitly is ended with a semicolon.</p>
<p>Thus the for loop consists of three parts and each part may be omitted. (Of course if you want to introduce a declaration in a for loop then it can not be omitted.:))</p>
<p>According to the original for loop then it correspinds to the following definition of the for loop in the C Standard</p>
<p>for ( expression<sub>opt</sub> ; expression<sub>opt</sub> ; expression<sub>opt</sub> ) statement</p>
<p>where the first expression is omitted because the variable <strong>iter</strong> used within the for loop is declared and initialized before the for loop</p>
<pre><code>    xcb_screen_iterator_t iter;

    iter = xcb_setup_roots_iterator (xcb_get_setup (c));
</code></pre>
<p>The second expression</p>
<pre><code>iter.rem;
</code></pre>
<p>specifies the condition of the for loop. If <strong>iter.rem</strong> is not equal to 0 then the loop continues its iterations and checks within its body whether the screen corresponding to the initial varlue of the parameter <strong>screen</strong> is found.</p>
<pre><code>if (screen == 0)
        return iter.data;
</code></pre>
<p>Otherwise the function returns NULL</p>
<pre><code>return NULL;
</code></pre>
<p>that is when the <strong>iter.rem</strong> used as the condition of the for loop is equal to 0 because the expression <strong>iter.rem</strong> is equivalent to the expression <strong>iter.rem != 0</strong>.</p>
<p>From the C24 Standard (6.8.5 Iteration statements):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>4 An iteration statement causes a statement called the loop body to be executed repeatedly until the controlling expression compares equal to 0. The repetition occurs regardless of whether the loop body is entered from the iteration statement or by a jump.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Pay attention to that</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An omitted expression-2 is replaced by a nonzero constant.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is for example the following for loop</p>
<pre><code>for (  ;;  )
</code></pre>
<p>is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>for ( ; 1; )
</code></pre>
<p>that in fact is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>while ( 1 )
</code></pre>
<p>The third expression of the for loop is an expression with the comma operator</p>
<pre><code>--screen, xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter)
</code></pre>
<p>It could be rewritten also like</p>
<pre><code>( --screen, xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter) )
</code></pre>
<p>That is the variable <strong>screen</strong> is decremented and the next iterator (<strong>iter</strong>) is calculated.</p>
<p>The loop could be rewritten alrenatively for example the following way (where all three parts of the loop are filled, the value of the original parameter screen is not decremented and the third expression is a simple expression with the post-increment operator):</p>
<pre><code>    xcb_screen_iterator_t iter;

    iter = xcb_setup_roots_iterator (xcb_get_setup (c));
    for (int i = 0; iter.rem; i++)
    {
      if (i == screen)
        return iter.data;
      else
        xcb_screen_next (&amp;iter);
    }

    return NULL;
</code></pre>

Q: Javascript drag over event doesn&#39;t work A: <p>You shouldn't prevent default in the <code>dragstart</code> function when using native JS for dragging. See here:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var drag = document.getElementById('drag'),
  drop = document.getElementById('dropped');

drag.addEventListener('dragstart', function(e) {
//  e.preventDefault();
  console.log('drag');
})

drop.addEventListener('drop', function(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  console.log(e);
  alert('dropped!');
})
drop.addEventListener('dragover', function(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>#dropped {
  background: #000;
  width: 400px;
  height: 400px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="drag" draggable="true"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipisicing elit. Animi eveniet illo, nisi placeat neque voluptatem sit ipsam corporis. Veritatis dicta perspiciatis temporibus error amet. Laborum, vero amet magnam nihil debitis!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id="dropped"&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>A little explanation about preventDefault() and where and why it should be used:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Calling the preventDefault method during both a dragenter and dragover event will indicate that a drop is allowed at that location. However, you will commonly wish to call the preventDefault method only in certain situations, for example, only if a link is being dragged. To do this, call a function which checks a condition and only cancels the event when the condition is met. If the condition is not met, don't cancel the event, and a drop will not occur there if the user releases the mouse button.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>A listener for the dragenter and dragover events are used to indicate valid drop targets, that is, places where dragged items may be dropped. Most areas of a web page or application are not valid places to drop data. Thus, the default handling for these events is to not allow a drop.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you want to allow a drop, you must prevent the default handling by cancelling the event. You can do this either by returning false from an attribute-defined event listener, or by calling the event's event.preventDefault method. The latter may be more feasible in a function defined in a separate script.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>In a web page, you should call the preventDefault method of the event if you have accepted the drop so that the default browser handling does not handle the dropped data as well. For example, when a link is dragged to a web page, Firefox will open the link. By cancelling the event, this behavior will be prevented.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>All the quotes taken from: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Guide/HTML/Drag_operations" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Guide/HTML/Drag_operations</a></p>

Q: is there a way to make it so event listener only handles one event at a time? A: <p>Step-by-step breakdown of exactly what was changed to make it work, and why those changes were necessary:**<br />
1. Added State Management (preventing &quot;spam-clicks&quot;).**</p>
<p><em><strong>1 step original problem:</strong></em> If a user rapidly clicked the button three times, it would trigger three separate, overlapping animations. Three different <code>setTimeout</code> functions would be counting down to 2200ms simultaneously, and when they fired, they would step all over each other, causing the progress bar to flash, reset abruptly, or get stuck.</p>
<p><em><strong>1 step fix:</strong></em></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong><code>let isAnimating = false;</code></strong>: A state variable was introduced to keep track of whether the animation is currently running.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>if (isAnimating) return;</code></strong>: If the button is clicked while it's already animating, the code immediately stops and ignores the click.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>button.disabled = true;</code></strong>: This provides visual and functional feedback to the user, letting the browser natively lock the button from being pressed again until the animation finishes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Fixing the CSS Transition Glitch:</strong></p>
<p><em><strong>2 step original problem:</strong></em>  To save processing power, Browsers batch DOM updates together. In original version, inside the <code>setTimeout</code>, you told the browser: &quot;Turn off transitions, jump to 0% width, turn transitions back on.&quot; The browser tried to execute all three of these at the exact same millisecond. As a result, it didn't register the &quot;jump to 0% width&quot; while transitions were off, and it awkwardly animated the bar shrinking backward.</p>
<p><em><strong>2 step fix:</strong></em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>void bar.offsetWidth;</code></strong>: This line is purely a hack to force the browser to pay attention. Whenever you ask JavaScript for the <code>offsetWidth</code> (or height, or margins) of an element, the browser is forced to pause, stop batching updates, and instantly calculate the layout to give you an accurate number (this is called triggering a &quot;Reflow&quot;). By forcing this reflow right after setting the width to 0%, you guarantee the browser physically shrinks the bar to 0% <em>before</em> it processes the line that turns the transition back on.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Fixing Invalid HTML (<code>&lt;div&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> inside <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> tag)</strong></p>
<p><em><strong>3 step original  problem:</strong></em> According to HTML5 standards, you cannot put a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>. While modern browsers try to be forgiving and render it anyway, it can lead to unpredictable styling quirks, accessibility issues, or weird click-target behavior.</p>
<p><em><strong>3 step fix:</strong></em></p>
<ul>
<li>The <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> tags were swapped out for <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> tags. <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> is an inline element, which is perfectly legal to nest inside a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Correcting CSS for the New HTML Tags</h3>
<p><em><strong>4 step problem (caused by Step 3):</strong></em> <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> tags are inline elements. By default, inline elements ignore <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> properties. If you just changed the tags to <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> without touching the CSS, your progress bar would disappear entirely.</p>
<p><em><strong>4 step fix:</strong></em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>display: block;</code></strong> was added to both <code>.progress-container</code> and <code>.progress-bar</code> in the CSS. This tells the browser, &quot;Treat these spans exactly like you would treat a div.&quot; It allows them to be legally nested inside the button, while still accepting the specific <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, and <code>margin</code> values required to draw the progress bar.</li>
</ul>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const button = document.getElementById("button");
const bar = document.getElementById("progressBar");

// var to track a state
let isAnimating = false;

button.addEventListener("click", function (event) {
    // if it's already animating, stop and ignore the click
    if (isAnimating) return;

    // set the flag to true to ignore future clicks
    isAnimating = true;

    // visually disable the button so the user knows they can't click it
    button.disabled = true; 

    bar.style.width = "100%";
    bar.style.opacity = "0%";

    setTimeout(() =&gt; {
        bar.style.transition = "none";
        bar.style.width = "0%";
        bar.style.opacity = "100%";

        /* Trick to remember: force the browser to register 0% width before turning transitions back on
         When you remove a CSS transition, change a value, and then immediately put the transition back, browsers sometimes group those commands together and glitch out, showing the bar shrinking backward. Asking JavaScript to check the offsetWidth forces the browser to apply the 0% width instantly before turning the transition back on.*/
        void bar.offsetWidth; 
        bar.style.transition = "";

        // reset the flag and re-enable the button
        isAnimating = false;
        button.disabled = false;
    }, 2200);
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.progress-container {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 500px;
  height: 12px;
  background-color: #e0e0e0;
  border-radius: 10px;
  overflow: hidden;
  margin: 8px auto;
  position: relative;
  display: block;
}
.progress-bar {
  width: 0%;
  height: 100%;
  background-color: #0071c5;
  background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #0071c5, #00aeef);
  border-radius: 10px;
  opacity: 100%;
  transition: width 1s linear, opacity 1s linear 1s;
  display: block;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button id="button"&gt;button
    &lt;span class="progress-container"&gt;
        &lt;span id="progressBar" class="progress-bar"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
